window.VIGIL_INDEX=[{"t": "Welcome", "u": "index.html", "s": "What Vigil is, who this guide is for, and how to use it.", "h": ["The idea in one picture", "Why a security problem?", "How to use this guide", "What this guide does not claim"], "x": "Learn GenAI by building a security agent Vigil is a small, real project that grows from one call to a language model into an autonomous agent that watches for security vulnerabilities , decides which ones matter, and follows up on them. You do not need to know any AI to start. You do need to be comfortable reading basic Python. Note This guide is a work in progress. Levels L0 to L2 are fully written. L3 to L6 are still being turned into guide pages; until then, each level file in levels/ has a long explanatory comment at the top, and the learning path lists everything. The idea in one picture Every level adds exactly one new idea on top of the one before it. By the end you will have seen, in running code, how an AI system turns into an AI agent . Why a security problem? Organisations face thousands of new CVEs a year and cannot fix them all. The useful question is not \"what is this CVE?\" but \"does it matter to us , and what do we do first?\". That question needs facts, judgement, tools and follow-up, which is exactly what makes it a good way to learn agents. How to use this guide 1. Read Getting started and get the code running (15 minutes, no API key needed for the first part). 2. Read How LLMs work . It covers just enough theory to follow the code. 3. Work through the levels in order. Each page follows the same shape: the question, the idea in plain English, a diagram, the real code, a run, experiments, mistakes to avoid, and a short self-check. 4. Do the matching exercise after each level. Use the glossary whenever a word is new. <div class=\"cards\" <a class=\"card\" href=\"pages/getting-started.html\" <em START</em <b Getting started</b <span Install, run the tests, run your first level.</span </a <a class=\"card\" href=\"pages/concepts.html\" <em THEORY</em <b How LLMs work</b <span Tokens, messages, temperature and cost in plain English.</span </a <a class=\"card\" href=\"pages/l0.html\" <em LEVEL 0</em <b A single call</b <span The one primitive every agent is built from.</span </a <a class=\"card\" href=\"pages/learning-path.html\" <em MAP</em <b Learning path</b <span Every level, notebook and exercise in order.</span </a </div What this guide does not claim Vigil is a teaching project. It shows the shape of real techniques with small, readable code. It is not a production security product, its cost figures are estimates, and the model's answers about a CVE can be wrong. Always check a model's claims against the official advisory before acting on them."}, {"t": "Getting started", "u": "pages/getting-started.html", "s": "Install Vigil, prove it works offline, then run your first level.", "h": ["What you need", "Step 1. Get the code and install it", "Step 2. Prove it works, offline", "Step 3. Look around with a notebook", "Step 4. Add your API key", "Step 5. Run Level 0", "If something goes wrong", "Optional: the web interface"], "x": "Getting started This takes about 15 minutes. Steps 1 to 3 need no API key and cost nothing , so you can confirm everything works before you spend a cent. What you need - Python 3.11 or newer. Check with python --version . - Git , to clone the repository. - An OpenAI API key , but only from step 4. Get one from the OpenAI platform. Running the levels uses a small amount of paid credit. - Docker is optional. You only need it for levels L5 and L6, which use a database. Step 1. Get the code and install it On macOS or Linux: On Windows PowerShell: Tip If PowerShell refuses to run the activate script, run Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned once, or skip activation and call .venv\\Scripts\\python.exe directly. A virtual environment is a private folder of Python packages for this project, so it cannot clash with anything else on your machine. Step 2. Prove it works, offline Every test replaces the real model with a stand-in, so this makes no network calls. You should see a long row of dots and a summary such as passed, skipped . A few tests are skipped when an optional package is missing, which is normal. Any failure on a fresh install is a bug worth reporting. Step 3. Look around with a notebook Open 00_setup.ipynb . The notebooks run fully offline and let you change one value at a time to see what happens. Step 4. Add your API key Open .env and set the key: Warning .env holds a secret. It is already listed in .gitignore , so git will not commit it. Never paste the key into code, chat, or a screenshot. Step 5. Run Level 0 You should see a header with the model name and CVE, then one panel containing a plain-English explanation of Log4Shell. The exact wording changes on every run; that is normal for a language model. Now open Level 0 and read the code next to the output you just produced. If something goes wrong What you see What it usually means --- --- The api_key client option must be set The key was not loaded. Check .env exists in the repo root and run from there. ModuleNotFoundError: vigil You skipped pip install -e ... or the virtual environment is not active. PowerShell blocks the activate script See the tip above. A 401 or insufficient_quota error The key is wrong, or the account has no credit. The README has a longer troubleshooting table. Optional: the web interface If you prefer clicking to typing, start the API and the Streamlit interface in two terminals: Then open the address Streamlit prints (normally http://localhost:8501 ). There is one page per level. Note Levels L5 and L6 store data in PostgreSQL. The simplest way to run them is docker compose up --build , which starts the API, the database and the interface together. Leave them until L0 to L4 feel comfortable."}, {"t": "How LLMs work", "u": "pages/concepts.html", "s": "Tokens, messages, temperature, context windows and cost, in just enough depth to read the code.", "h": ["1. A language model predicts the next piece of text", "2. Tokens", "3. Messages and roles", "4. Temperature", "5. Cost and latency", "What a model call looks like", "Check yourself"], "x": "How LLMs work (just enough) You do not need the maths. You need five ideas, because every level in Vigil is built from them. 1. A language model predicts the next piece of text A large language model is a program trained on a huge amount of text. Given some text, it predicts what should come next, one small piece at a time, until it decides to stop. Everything else, such as answering questions, writing code or following instructions, comes out of doing that very well. Two consequences matter for Vigil: - The model does not look things up . It answers from patterns it learned during training, so it can be confidently wrong about a specific CVE. Later levels fix this by giving it tools . - The model does not remember earlier calls. Each call starts blank. Anything it should know, you must send again (L5 is about doing that well). 2. Tokens Models read and write tokens , not letters or words. A token is a chunk of text, roughly four characters or three quarters of a word in English. <div class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" <div class=\"node\" <b \"Log4Shell is critical\"</b <small your text</small </div <span class=\"arr\" →</span <div class=\"node\" Log</div <div class=\"node\" 4</div <div class=\"node\" Shell</div <div class=\"node\" is</div <div class=\"node\" critical</div </div The split above is only an illustration; real tokenizers cut text in their own ways. Tokens matter because you are billed per token and because every model has a context window , a maximum number of tokens it can handle in one call (your input plus its reply). The notebook 01_tokenization.ipynb lets you see this directly. 3. Messages and roles You do not send the model a single string. You send a list of messages , each with a role : Role Who writes it What it is for --- --- --- system You, the developer The job description: who the model is and how it should behave user You or your end user The actual task or question assistant The model Its earlier replies, which you send back to continue a conversation In code that is just a list of dictionaries: This list is the atomic unit of every level in Vigil. Even the autonomous agent in L6 is, underneath, building and sending lists like this. 4. Temperature Temperature controls how adventurous the next-token choice is. - Low (0.0 to 0.3) : the model sticks to the most likely wording. Answers are consistent. Good for facts and structured data. - High (0.8 and above) : the model takes less likely paths. Answers vary and feel more creative. Good for brainstorming, risky for facts. Vigil uses low values (0.1 to 0.2) because a security team wants steady, careful answers. Even at 0.0 you cannot assume identical output every time. 5. Cost and latency A call has a price and a delay, and both grow with the number of tokens in and out. That is why later levels care about prompt size, caching and picking a cheaper model for easy jobs. Note Vigil shows a cost per run, but it is an estimate from list prices stored in vigil/pricing.py . Provider prices change and are not synced, so treat the number as a rough guide, not a bill. What a model call looks like The reply also reports how many tokens went in and out. Vigil adds these up to give each run its token and cost summary. Check yourself ??? Why can a model be confidently wrong about a specific CVE? It answers from patterns in its training data, not from a live database. If it never saw reliable details about that CVE, or saw them only in passing, it may produce a fluent answer that is wrong. Tools and retrieval (the F-series) exist to ground it in real data. ??? A call fails because the input is too long. What limit did you hit? The context window: the maximum tokens for input plus output in one call. ??? Why is temperature set low for a security analysis? You want consistent, careful answers. High temperature trades consistency for variety, which is the wrong trade for facts. Ready for code? Continue to Level 0 ."}, {"t": "L0: A single call", "u": "pages/l0.html", "s": "One prompt in, one answer out. The primitive every agent is built from.", "h": ["The idea in plain English", "Part 1: the job description", "Part 2: the call", "Part 3: the task prompt", "Run it", "What the code also does", "Try it", "Common mistakes", "Check yourself"], "x": "Level 0: A single call The question this level answers: how do I get a language model to do one useful thing from code? Every system in this project, including the autonomous agent at L6, is built on this one move: send messages to a model, read the reply. Understand it well and the rest is composition. The idea in plain English You hire a specialist for one job. First you hand over a job description (the system prompt ). Then you hand over the task (the user prompt ). They write back. That is all L0 does. Part 1: the job description The system prompt sets the model's role, tone and limits. Specific beats generic every time. Look at what it does: - Defines a role : \"senior cybersecurity analyst\". This shapes the model's point of view. - Sets a style : \"plain English, not jargon\". - Draws a boundary : \"do not provide exploit code\". Safety rules belong here. - Allows honesty : \"if you are uncertain... say so\". This reduces confident guessing. Compare with the weak version, \"You are a helpful assistant.\" . The model would still answer, but with no angle, no limits and no guidance. Part 2: the call Here is the whole function. Read the comments, which explain each choice. The part that matters is this call: Piece What it does Why this value --- --- --- model Which model answers Read from OPENAI_MODEL , defaulting to gpt-4o-mini , a cheap, fast model that is good enough here messages The conversation so far System prompt first, then the task temperature How adventurous the wording is 0.2 keeps factual answers steady max_tokens A cap on the reply length 600 tokens is roughly 450 words, enough for a clear summary and a hard stop on runaway cost The reply is an object, not a string. The text is at response.choices 0 .message.content . The 0 is there because the API can return several alternative answers; you asked for one, so you take the first. Part 3: the task prompt The user prompt inside explain_cve is specific, structured and bounded . It asks five numbered questions instead of \"tell me about this CVE\". Numbered questions produce answers that are easier to read and easier to compare between runs. Run it You should see a header showing the model and CVE, then a panel with the explanation. Check it against the five questions in the prompt. Does it answer all five? Warning Do not trust the details blindly. The model answers from memory, not from a live database. Compare version numbers and severity with the official advisory. Level 4 fixes this by letting the model look things up. What the code also does Three small helpers sit above the main function: _reset_usage , _track and get_usage . They add up the tokens used in a run and turn them into an estimated cost. The API and the web interface read these numbers. You can ignore them while learning the core idea. Try it Try it Change one thing at a time and re-run. Write down what changed. 1) Set temperature to 0.0 , then 1.0 , and run the same CVE three times each. Which setting gives more varied answers? 2) Replace SYSTEM_PROMPT with \"You are a helpful assistant.\" and compare the quality. 3) Lower max_tokens to 60 and see where the answer is cut off. The notebook 02_prompting.ipynb runs experiments like these offline, and the first exercise lets you practise building the message list yourself: Common mistakes - A vague system prompt. You get a generic answer. Be specific about role, style and limits. - Treating the reply as a fact. It is a draft from a model that can be wrong. - No token cap. Long replies cost more and can run on. Always set max_tokens . - Expecting identical output each run. Even at low temperature the wording can vary. Design code that tolerates that. - Forgetting L0 has no memory. A second call knows nothing about the first. Check yourself ??? What are the two messages sent in L0, and what is each for? A system message that gives the model its role and rules, and a user message that carries the specific task. ??? Why is temperature 0.2 and not 1.0 here? A security analysis should be steady and careful. A high value would make answers more varied and more likely to wander. ??? What is the main weakness of L0 that later levels address? It is one call from memory: no structure you can program against , no specialists , no live data , no memory . Next: Level 1 chains calls together and makes the output machine-readable."}, {"t": "L1: Chaining and structure", "u": "pages/l1.html", "s": "Three focused calls in sequence, each feeding the next, with typed output instead of free text.", "h": ["Idea 1: a chain beats one big prompt", "Idea 2: structured output", "The helper that does the work", "The chain itself", "Run it", "Try it", "Common mistakes", "Check yourself"], "x": "Level 1: Chaining and structure The question this level answers: how do I get reliable, usable results instead of one long paragraph? L0 returned a block of prose. A person can read that, but your code cannot reliably act on it. L1 fixes two problems at once: it splits one big task into a chain of small ones , and it makes each step return typed data . Idea 1: a chain beats one big prompt If you ask one prompt to \"explain the CVE, assess the risk, and write a remediation plan\", each part gets a fraction of the model's attention. Three focused prompts do better, and each can use what the one before it found. The output of step 1 becomes part of the input to step 2, and steps 1 and 2 both feed step 3. This is called context accumulation . Idea 2: structured output Free text is awkward for code. If the model returns \"the severity seems quite high, maybe 9ish\", what do you store in a database? With structured output you describe the shape you want, and the model must return data in exactly that shape. Vigil describes shapes with Pydantic classes. Here is step 1's shape: Notice what the types do for you: - Literal \"Critical\", \"High\", \"Medium\", \"Low\" means the model can only answer with one of those exact words. No \"CRITCAL\", no \"high\". - Field(ge=0.0, le=10.0) keeps the score between 0 and 10. - list str and bool give you real lists and true/false values to program against. Steps 2 and 3 have their own shapes, RiskAssessment and RemediationPlan . The urgency in step 2 is a fixed set of choices such as \"Patch immediately\" or \"Patch within 7 days\", so downstream code can sort and filter on it. The helper that does the work One small function wraps every call in the chain: Two details are worth understanding: - response_format with a JSON schema tells the model to return JSON that matches your class. The key line is \"strict\": True . - model_validate ) turns that JSON back into a real Python object and checks it. If anything is off, you get an error instead of silent bad data. The difference from L0 is simple. L0 returns a string. call_llm returns an object, so you write result.severity and result.cvss_score instead of parsing text. The chain itself Step 2 receives the whole of step 1's output. Read how it builds its prompt from the previous result: The prompt is not \"assess CVE-2021-44228\". It contains the facts step 1 established: the product, versions, attack vector and score. The risk assessment is informed by the summary. Step 3 does the same with both earlier results. Run it You should see three stages printed in order: a CVE summary, a risk assessment, then a remediation plan. Check that the plan reflects the risk. A critical, easy-to-exploit CVE should produce urgent immediate actions. Try it Try it 1) In step2_assess_risk , delete the lines that pass the summary in and re-run. Does the assessment get worse or more generic? That difference is the value of chaining. 2) Add a new Literal option to urgency and see whether the model uses it. 3) Make a field optional by giving it a default and note when it comes back empty. The exercise exercises/l1_chaining practises threading one step's output into the next: Common mistakes - Making steps too big. If a step does three jobs, split it. - Passing too little forward. A later step can only use what you give it. - Passing too much forward. Every extra token costs money and can distract the model. Pass what the next step needs. - Trusting the type, not the content. The shape is guaranteed; the facts are still the model's, so they can be wrong. - Forgetting the cost. Three calls cost roughly three times one. Chains buy quality, not savings. Check yourself ??? What is context accumulation? Each step receives the useful results of earlier steps, so later steps build on earlier findings instead of starting from nothing. ??? Why use Literal types for severity? It limits the model to a fixed set of exact values, which removes typos and variations and lets your code branch on the result safely. ??? What does structured output guarantee, and what does it not? It guarantees the shape: the right fields and types. It does not guarantee that the content is true. Next: Level 2 runs several specialists at the same time."}, {"t": "L2: Parallel agents", "u": "pages/l2.html", "s": "Four specialists analyse the same CVE at once, then a moderator makes the call.", "h": ["The idea in plain English", "What is an \"agent\" here?", "Running one agent", "Why parallel? A short timing story", "async and asyncio.gather, in plain terms", "Run it", "Try it", "Common mistakes", "Check yourself"], "x": "Level 2: Parallel agents The question this level answers: what if several different experts should look at the same problem, and I do not want to wait for them one at a time? In L1 each step depended on the one before it, so they had to run in order. Some work has no such dependency. How exploitable a CVE is does not depend on its business impact, so those two analyses can run at the same time . The idea in plain English You convene a small committee. Four specialists each read the same file and write their own report independently. A chair then reads all four reports and delivers the verdict. This pattern is called fan-out then fan-in . The fan-out is parallel. The final synthesis is sequential, because the moderator needs all four reports first. What is an \"agent\" here? At this level an agent is nothing mysterious. It is three things: 1. a system prompt that defines a specialist role, 2. a schema that defines the shape of its report, and 3. a function that calls the model with both. Same model, same API, different persona. The four agents are defined as plain data: Each one has its own report class, such as ExploitabilityReport and ImpactReport , so each speaks about its own specialty in typed fields (the same structured-output idea you met in Level 1 ). Running one agent The new words are async and await . They let Python start a slow job, such as waiting on the model, and do other work while it waits, instead of freezing. If you have not met them before, read the next section slowly. Why parallel? A short timing story If each call takes about three seconds: Approach Total time --- --- One after another (L1 style) about 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 12 seconds All at once (this level) about 3 seconds The moderator call is added after, because it must wait for all four. You pay for the same number of tokens either way; parallelism saves time , not money. These timings are an illustration, and real latency varies. async and asyncio.gather , in plain terms A normal function call blocks: nothing else happens until it returns. An async function can pause at an await and let others run while it waits for the network. asyncio.gather takes several of these and starts them all together. It returns once every one has finished, with results in the same order you passed them in. Here is the whole pipeline: Find these three beats in the code: 1. Fan-out. asyncio.gather( run_agent(agent, cve_id) for agent in AGENTS ) starts all four agents. 2. Collect. The results become a dictionary keyed by agent name. 3. Synthesise. run_moderator receives all four reports as its context and returns a FinalVerdict : priority, a patch deadline in days, an executive summary, three actions and a confidence level. Run it You should see a line saying four agents were launched in parallel, then a time for how long they took, then one card per agent and a verdict from the moderator. Compare the reported time with what four separate calls would take. Note Agents can disagree. The Patch agent may call the work easy while the Business agent calls the risk severe. That is useful, not a bug: the moderator's job is to weigh them. Try it Try it 1) Add a fifth agent, for example a \"Compliance Agent\", by adding one entry and one report class. Notice how little code a new specialist needs. 2) Time the run, then change asyncio.gather into a plain for loop with await inside and compare the time. 3) Give two agents deliberately conflicting system prompts and read how the moderator handles it. Common mistakes - Parallelising dependent work. If step B needs step A's output, they cannot run together. - Calling await one at a time in a loop. That is sequential again. Collect the coroutines and use asyncio.gather . - Too many agents at once. Providers limit how many requests you can send per minute. A large fan-out can hit those limits. - Assuming more agents means more truth. Four agents share one model and one training set, so they can share the same blind spot. - Forgetting the cost. Five calls cost about five times one call. Check yourself ??? Why can the four agents run in parallel but the moderator cannot? The agents are independent of each other. The moderator needs all four reports as input, so it must wait. ??? Does running in parallel reduce the cost? No. The same tokens are used. It reduces the waiting time. ??? In this level, what is an agent? A system prompt, an output schema and a function that calls the model with them. It does not decide what to do next yet; that comes with routing and tools . Next up: routing , where the system decides which specialists to call. That page is not written yet; see the learning path and the heavily commented levels/l3_routing.py ."}, {"t": "Learning path", "u": "pages/learning-path.html", "s": "Every level, notebook and exercise in the order to do them.", "h": ["Core path", "Then go further", "How to learn from each level", "When you get stuck"], "x": "Learning path Do the Core path first. It is enough to understand how an AI system becomes an agent. The rest goes deeper and can be done in any order that interests you. Core path Step Do this Time Guide page --- --- --- --- 1 Install and run the tests 15 min Getting started 2 Read the theory 20 min How LLMs work 3 Notebook 00_setup.ipynb 15 min notebooks/ 4 L0 : a single call 30 min L0 5 Exercise l0_prompting 20 min exercises/l0_prompting 6 L1 : chaining and structured output 45 min L1 7 Exercise l1_chaining 20 min exercises/l1_chaining 8 L2 : parallel agents 45 min L2 9 L3 : routing 45 min not written yet, read levels/l3_routing.py 10 L4 : tool use 60 min not written yet, read levels/l4_tool_use.py 11 Exercise l4_tool_dispatch 30 min exercises/l4_tool_dispatch The times are rough estimates for a first read, not measurements. Then go further Topic Where to look --- --- Memory and feedback loops levels/l5_memory.py (needs PostgreSQL) Autonomous monitoring levels/l6_autonomous.py (needs PostgreSQL) Reading PDFs and images levels/l4b_multimodal.py Agent designs: ReAct, plan-and-execute, reflection, multi-agent architectures/ Making agents safe and reliable: guardrails, evaluation, observability, cost control reliability/ Retrieval, tokenization, prompting, privacy and more levels/foundations/ and the notebooks The full feature list and its status specs/progress-board.md Every module, function and endpoint Code reference Tip Tokenization, prompting and embeddings each have a runnable notebook: 01_tokenization , 02_prompting and 03_embeddings_rag . They are the quickest way to build intuition, because you change a value and watch the result. How to learn from each level 1. Read the guide page first so you know what to look for. 2. Run the level and read its output. 3. Open the level's Python file. The comments at the top explain the concepts. 4. Change one thing and run it again. 5. Do the exercise, if there is one. 6. Answer the self-check questions out loud. When you get stuck Search the guide with the box at the top (press / ), check the glossary , then the troubleshooting table in the project README."}, {"t": "Glossary", "u": "pages/glossary.html", "s": "Plain-English definitions of the words used in this project.", "h": [], "x": "Glossary Short definitions, in alphabetical order. Where a word is used in the code, the level that introduces it is shown in brackets. Term Meaning --- --- Agent A system where the model chooses what to do next, often using tools, until a goal is met. At L2 an \"agent\" is only a prompt plus a schema; real autonomy arrives in L4 to L6. API The way your code talks to a service. Here, mainly the model provider's API, and also the FastAPI service Vigil exposes. API key A secret string that identifies and bills your account. Keep it in .env , never in code. async / await Python syntax that lets a program wait on slow work, such as a network call, without freezing. Used from L2. Chain Several model calls in sequence where each feeds the next . Context window The maximum number of tokens a model can handle in one call, counting your input and its reply. Context accumulation Passing earlier results into later steps so they build on what is known . CVE Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures: a public identifier for a security flaw, such as CVE-2021-44228. CVSS A 0 to 10 severity score for a vulnerability. EPSS A score estimating how likely a vulnerability is to be exploited soon (used at L4). Embedding A list of numbers that represents the meaning of a text, so similar texts are close together. Used for memory and retrieval. Evaluation Measuring how good an AI system's answers are against a known set of examples. Fan-out / fan-in Sending one input to many workers at once, then combining their outputs . Guardrail A check that blocks or fixes unsafe or invalid input and output . Hallucination A fluent answer that is not true. A model can invent details. LLM Large language model: a model trained on lots of text to predict the next token. MCP Model Context Protocol: a standard way for a model to discover and use outside tools. Memory Information kept between runs so the system can recall earlier work . A model itself has none. Moderator An extra step that reads other agents' outputs and produces one verdict . NVD The US National Vulnerability Database, a public source of CVE data (used at L4). Orchestration The code that decides which calls happen, in what order. Prompt The text and messages you send to the model. Pydantic A Python library for describing and validating data shapes. Used for structured output. RAG Retrieval-augmented generation: look up relevant documents first, then give them to the model so it answers from real sources. Routing Choosing which path or specialist handles an input . Schema A description of the exact fields and types you expect back. Structured output Making the model return data that matches a schema instead of free text . System prompt The instruction that sets the model's role and rules . Temperature A setting for how varied the model's wording is. Low is steady, high is varied . Token A chunk of text, roughly three quarters of an English word. Billing and limits are counted in tokens. Tool use Letting the model ask your code to run a function, such as looking up a CVE, and then using the result . User prompt The message carrying the actual task . Vector See embedding."}];